Digital Design and Synthesis Fundamentals

1. Introduction

Digital hardware design is an important part of Very Large Scale Integration (VLSI). It involves describing, verifying, and implementing digital circuits using hardware description languages and electronic design automation tools.

Register Transfer Level (RTL) design describes how data moves between registers and how logical operations are performed on that data. Verilog HDL is commonly used to describe digital hardware at the RTL level.

This session introduces the basic RTL design flow, functional simulation, waveform analysis, and logic synthesis.

2. Objectives

The main objectives of Day 1 are:

- Understand the fundamentals of RTL design.
- Learn the role of Verilog HDL in digital hardware development.
- Understand the purpose of a testbench.
- Simulate a digital design and inspect its output.
- Learn the basic functions of Icarus Verilog and GTKWave.
- Understand RTL synthesis using Yosys.
- Identify the relationship between RTL code and a synthesized netlist.

3. RTL Design Flow

A typical RTL design flow consists of the following stages:

1. Understand the design requirements.
2. Write the RTL description using Verilog HDL.
3. Develop a testbench to apply input signals.
4. Simulate the design and check its functionality.
5. Inspect waveforms and verify the outputs.
6. Synthesize the RTL using a synthesis tool.
7. Examine the generated netlist and synthesis statistics.

This process helps designers verify functionality and understand how the RTL description is converted into a hardware representation.

4. Introduction to Verilog HDL

Verilog HDL is a hardware description language used to model digital systems. It supports the description of combinational logic, sequential circuits, registers, counters, multiplexers, and more complex digital components.

A Verilog design is commonly organized into modules. Each module can have input ports, output ports, internal signals, and logic describing its behavior.

5. Example – 2:1 Multiplexer

A multiplexer selects one of two input signals based on a select signal.

Verilog Design

module mux2to1 (
    input a,
    input b,
    input sel,
    output y
);
    assign y = sel ? b : a;
endmodule

Working Principle

- When "sel = 0", output "y" follows input "a".
- When "sel = 1", output "y" follows input "b".

The conditional operator selects the appropriate input according to the value of the select signal.

6. Truth Table

A| B| SEL| Y
