7. Functional Simulation

Functional simulation checks whether the design produces the expected outputs for the input conditions applied by a testbench.

A testbench instantiates the design under test, generates input signals, and observes the outputs.

The simulation process helps identify coding errors before synthesis.

8. Tools Used

Icarus Verilog

An open-source tool used to compile and simulate supported Verilog designs.

GTKWave

A waveform viewer used to inspect signal values and transitions over simulation time.

Yosys

An open-source synthesis tool used to process RTL descriptions, optimize logic, and generate netlists.

9. Basic Simulation Commands

Assuming the design is saved as "mux2to1.v" and its testbench as "tb_mux2to1.v":

iverilog -o mux_sim mux2to1.v tb_mux2to1.v
vvp mux_sim

These commands compile the Verilog files and execute the resulting simulation.

To view waveforms, the testbench must also generate a waveform file, such as a VCD file, using "$dumpfile" and "$dumpvars". The resulting file can then be opened in GTKWave.

10. Introduction to RTL Synthesis

RTL synthesis translates a supported RTL description into a logic implementation represented by a netlist.

During synthesis, the tool processes the design, infers hardware structures, and performs logic optimizations. The result depends on the RTL description, synthesis settings, and target technology.

Example Yosys Commands

read_verilog mux2to1.v
hierarchy -top mux2to1
synth -top mux2to1
stat
write_verilog mux2to1_netlist.v

These commands read the source file, select the top-level module, synthesize the design, display statistics, and write a synthesized Verilog netlist.

11. RTL and Netlist

RTL describes the intended hardware behavior at a higher level of abstraction. A synthesized netlist describes the resulting interconnected logic elements.

Examining both representations helps designers understand how their code is translated into hardware.

12. Applications

RTL design and synthesis are used in the development of:

- Digital controllers.
- Communication systems.
- Processor components.
- Memory controllers.
- Signal-processing circuits.
- Application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs).
- Field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs).

13. Conclusion

Day 1 introduces the basic principles of RTL design, Verilog HDL, functional simulation, waveform analysis, and synthesis.

The 2:1 multiplexer example demonstrates how a simple digital circuit can be described using Verilog and examined through simulation and synthesis. These fundamentals provide a foundation for the advanced topics covered in subsequent workshop sessions.

